The VitaKernel-Livre subproject is licensed under The VitaSet License
and may contain code that is dedicated to the Public Domain.

The VitaKernel-Livre will be written primarily in the Vita Programming
Language by VitaSet LLC, but the Vita Compiler Collection can also
compile inline assembly and inline bitfields along with the code
that is written in the Vita Programming Language by VitaSet LLC.

Trusted Computing Base: The VitaKernel-Livre, which is composed of
these Subsystems: VitaClip, VitaUI, VitaEdit, VitaFiles, VitaCrypt,
VitaCompress, VitaMount, Vita Compiler Collection, VitaVM.
9 Subsystems, all in Ring 0. All in one PE32+ file, signed for
UEFI® Secure Boot. The VitaKernel-Livre is designed for the x86_64
Multicore Architecture. A future port to another architecture
could be made when I can get my Vita FPGA computer chips made,
then I can make my Vita Computer.

To run an arbitrary program, compile and link the VitaKernel-Livre
source code and the program source code together using the VitaEdit
program, accessible from the VitaUI. This creates a VitaVM Image.

Then, sign the VitaVM Image with a VitaKernel-Livre Asymmetric Signing
Key using the VitaFiles program, accessible from the VitaUI.

Then, run it in a VitaVM with access to one or more directories in one
or more filesystems, using the VitaVM program, accessible from the
VitaUI.

To edit the Trusted Computing Base Source Code or arbitrary source
code, use the VitaEdit program, accessible from the VitaUI and press
the Save button from time to time.

To recompile the Trusted Computing Base Source Code or arbitrary source
code, with or without changes, use the VitaEdit program, accessible
from the VitaUI and press the Vita Compiler Collection button.

To sign, verify, encrypt or decrypt files, directories and binaries
with a selected asymmetric key, use the VitaFiles program, accessible
from the VitaUI and press the VitaCrypt button. From there, follow the
pop-up instructions for signing, verifying, encrypting and decrypting
files, directories and binaries. You can import asymmetric keys and you
can create your own asymmetric keys.

To copy, delete, move, compress, decompress files, directories and
binaries and mount and unmount drives, use the VitaFiles program,
accessible from the VitaUI. Remember to make backups from time to time.

To interact with the VitaClip program, press Ctrl + C on selected text
to copy it into the clipboard, press Ctrl + V to paste from the
clipboard, answer “Yes” or “No” with the mouse or keyboard to let a
VitaVM read the clipboard, or press on the VitaClip program, accessible
from the VitaUI.

The VitaUI has a button that shows the choices of shutdown, reboot,
sleep and hibernate.

All of the Subsystems are multicore, but a VitaVM can run with fewer
cores. A VitaVM uses all cores by default.

The VitaOS-Livre Operating System and the VitaKernel-Livre subproject
is a hobby by VitaSet LLC.

A VitaVM Image can be an entire Operating System, not just a computer
program.

